TEXT 248 -232 Left 3 ;#56 Real world inductor: winding resistance in series, interwinding capacitance across
TEXT 248 -176 Left 2 ;Source: Alan Wolke (W2AEW), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vi24SpKYYoQ
TEXT 248 -136 Left 2 ;Alan's real world inductor is Rs in series with L, with the interwinding capacitance Cw across\nthe pair. Sweeping it shows the parallel self-resonance above which the coil looks capacitive.
TEXT 856 48 Left 2 !.ac dec 500 10k 1G
TEXT 856 104 Left 2 !.meas ac Z_MAX MAX mag(V(Z))
TEXT 856 144 Left 2 !.meas ac Z_AT_FR FIND mag(V(Z)) AT=22.51Meg
TEXT 856 184 Left 2 !.meas ac Z_AT_1MEG FIND mag(V(Z)) AT=1Meg
TEXT 856 224 Left 2 !.meas ac Z_AT_200MEG FIND mag(V(Z)) AT=200Meg
TEXT 856 312 Left 2 ;I1 is a 1 A AC probe, so V(Z) reads\ndirectly as the impedance of the coil,\nin ohms.
TEXT 856 424 Left 2 ;Alan draws this model but gives no values for it, so\n10 uH and 5 pF are illustrative choices for a small RF\nchoke. The 25 ohm Rs stands for the winding resistance\nplus the skin-effect loss Alan describes, and it sets\nthe height of the peak.
TEXT 248 648 Left 2 ;Below resonance V(Z) rises like an inductor, peaks at the parallel self-resonance near\n22.5 MHz, then falls like a capacitor. Q at a given frequency is XL divided by Rs.
